{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion","id":330032,"title":"Sell me on Wr20/Wraith the Oblivion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-04T05:20:44-08:00","id":330032,"title":"Sell me on Wr20/Wraith the Oblivion","content":"Hi all,<br /> <br /> So back during the M20 Kickstarter Campaign I started a thread about being \u201csold\u201d on the concept of the game (link below):<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/90822-sell-me-on-m20-mage-the-ascension\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/90822-sell-me-on-m20-mage-the-ascension\"> Sell me on M20/Mage the Ascension - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/90822-sell-me-on-m20-mage-the-ascension\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/90822-sell-me-on-m20-mage-the-ascension </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Hi all. So this is pretty much what it says on the tin for the most part. Some context... I'm a long time WoD player (around a decade) but its been about four years since I played anything in the Classic World of Darkness. Since then I've been firmly entrenched in the NWoD. Recently after a few years of a </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I intended it as a sorta casual thread, but it ended up being a really interesting thread with a lot of conversations I found really engaging. So I thought I would do the same thing for Wraith now that he Kickstarter has started.<br /> <br /> My CWoD experience is limited to Vampire the Masquerade/Dark Ages. So I\u2019m a complete layman when it comes to Wraith. I basically know it mostly takes place in the Underworld and that its pretty grim-dark that\u2019s about it. All my \u201cghost\u201d knowledge is pretty much filtered through Vampiric interaction with ghosts and the underworld. So the Necromancy discipline and some True Black Hand fluff.<br /> <br /> I have some experiences with Wraiths NWoD equivalent Geist, but I am well aware that the two games are about as thematically different as it\u2019s possible to get. Or at least that\u2019s the impression I\u2019ve absorbed from the general conversations about the two on the forums. However, I am interested in the contrast between the two from the experience of people who have played both games. <br /> <br /> To be a bit clearer about what I\u2019m kinda curious about and want to see explored in this thread: I'm interested in people\u2019s thoughts about Oblivion as well as just a basic explanation of the game. After all, I can look up the detail on Wiki and TvTropes anytime, not to mention the Kickstarter has done a good job of detailing the basic details via the preview docs. The point is I really want people\u2019s thoughts about Oblivion.<br /> <br /> Issues like:<br /> What makes it a fun game for people?<br /> What good experiences (and not so good experiences/problems) have people had with the game?<br /> What do people think are the overarching themes of Oblivion?<br /> What sort of stories the game supports? <br /> How does it differ from other CWoD games (beyond the obvious splat change) and how is it similar? (Both in general themes and in specific setting ideas)<br /> <br /> These are just examples, but they are the sort of thing that I think demonstrate the &quot;core&quot; of a game and what it means to different players. <br /> <br /> I also have four sorta more specific questions:<br /> <br /> 1) Just how depressing is this game? Considering I\u2019ve heard it referred to as \u201cthe Grey Book of Depression\u201d in previous conversations on this forum.<br /> <br /> 2) How has the whole Shadow play thing worked out for people in practice? In theory I get that it\u2019s a kinda need idea. From the anecdotes of the one group I know who have played Wraith before; it was a nightmare to actually implement.<br /> <br /> 3) How does Wraith fit into the \u201cUrban Horror/Fantasy\u201d of the WoD in regards to the fact that the majority of the game takes place in another realm removed from mortals? Something I have always liked about the WoD is the \u201cthrough a mirror darkly\u201d aspect of the setting, so I\u2019m wondering how something that steers more towards outright fantasy might work and still be part of the WoD. <br /> <br /> 4) What is the \u201cmorality\u201d of Wraith? Is it very \u201cshaded of moral ambiguity\u201d or more \u201cblack and white\u201d in its take on things? I usually build stories around antagonists that drive them, so I\u2019m a big believer in needing a good villain. Hence I\u2019m kinda interested in who the \u201cbad guys\u201d are in the Wraith setting (both on a minor/personal and major/existential scale.<br /> <br /> Well, that\u2019s all for now.<br /> <br /> I would like to thank everyone that takes the time to reply in this thread. Let awesome conversation being!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-04T05:41:41-08:00","id":330043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1) Just how depressing is this game? Considering I\u2019ve heard it referred to as \u201cthe Grey Book of Depression\u201d in previous conversations on this forum. </div> </div> </div>Wraith society is basically hell : A tyranny where every crime can result to an eternity of having your consciousness imprisoned in a sword or other objects. Slavery is endemic and quasi-legal and you can leave the world in horrible circumstances and wake up in chains or on the auction block.<br /> <br /> Setting-wise horrible events in history are explored, I mean there are books on First World War and the Holocaust out there<br /> <br /> Homewer I have never thought of the game as more depressing as Vampire &quot;I am a monster and will become worse as the centuries pass&quot;, Werewolf &quot;The devil is winning the War in Heavens&quot;, Mage &quot;Cosmic power brought low by human pettiness&quot; or Changeling &quot;You're going to weaken and die&quot;<br /> <br /> The main difference is Wraith has a clear victory path. Your character can solve the issues which tie him to the living world and Transcend to whatever place human souls go. You can make peace with your darker urges, understand them and shackle them to your own good.<br /> <br /> Society-wise Wraith is the most horrible of the World of Darkness, Indiviually-wise this is the most hopeful","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2225-ganonso","name":"ganonso","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2225_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-04T09:13:21-08:00","id":330224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Issues like:<br /> What makes it a fun game for people?<br /> What good experiences (and not so good experiences/problems) have people had with the game?<br /> What do people think are the overarching themes of Oblivion?<br /> What sort of stories the game supports?<br /> How does it differ from other CWoD games (beyond the obvious splat change) and how is it similar? (Both in general themes and in specific setting ideas)</div> </div> </div> </div>First off I should point out I've never had a chance to play WtO, I've read plenty of WtO and Orpheus but I've never had a chance to play it.<br /> <br /> That said WtO can be a very interesting, if different, game. What makes it fun? Do you like ghost stories? Wanna try it from the other side? Want to have tense adventures into bizarre, impossible places, encountering dangerous monsters? Want to explore themes of spirituality? The ideas of life and death? Play games of politics without involving mortal governments?<br /> <br /> The overarching themes? Generally WtO has many of the same themes of other OWoD games but its biggest themes are those of spirituality, life and death, what do you when bad things happen to you and what you will do address the baggage you've acquired over your life.<br /> <br /> There are two really good WtO review videos from the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MarTvlktcXA\" target=\"_blank\">Gentlemen Gamer</a> and the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JN1vXuAtoOI\" target=\"_blank\">Grumpy Celt</a> and I recommend giving them a watch.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Just how depressing is this game? Considering I\u2019ve heard it referred to as \u201cthe Grey Book of Depression\u201d in previous conversations on this forum.<br /> <br /> 2) How has the whole Shadow play thing worked out for people in practice? In theory I get that it\u2019s a kinda need idea. From the anecdotes of the one group I know who have played Wraith before; it was a nightmare to actually implement.<br /> <br /> 3) How does Wraith fit into the \u201cUrban Horror/Fantasy\u201d of the WoD in regards to the fact that the majority of the game takes place in another realm removed from mortals? Something I have always liked about the WoD is the \u201cthrough a mirror darkly\u201d aspect of the setting, so I\u2019m wondering how something that steers more towards outright fantasy might work and still be part of the WoD.<br /> <br /> 4) What is the \u201cmorality\u201d of Wraith? Is it very \u201cshaded of moral ambiguity\u201d or more \u201cblack and white\u201d in its take on things? I usually build stories around antagonists that drive them, so I\u2019m a big believer in needing a good villain. Hence I\u2019m kinda interested in who the \u201cbad guys\u201d are in the Wraith setting (both on a minor/personal and major/existential scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) It can be very, very bleak and depressing. Stygia can very from relatively benign albeit corrupt and bloated government to a horribly corrupt and monstrous empire. And your own worst enemy is in your head. That said there is an undercurrent of hope within the line. Even in the grimest book of the line which talks about wraiths created by the Holocaust there is an undercurrent of hope. For instance, one group of Holocaust wraiths are trying to redeem Spectres, wraiths gone mad by their pain and Shadows, while another, at the time of the publication, was trying to ensure the construction of a monument on the site of their mass graves so that their still living families and others can come and remember them which creates Pathos to sustain the wraiths.<br /> <br /> And of course there is Transcendence, only achievable end of the OWoD lines. With vampires, no matter how much power you achieve there's always a bigger fish out there. Werewolf, your enemy is more numerous and powerful than you and pretty much have already won. But with WtO you can resolve your unfinished business and escape the nightmare of the Underworld, moving on as you should have.<br /> <br /> 2) Haven't played, can't comment much on it. But I've heard it can be difficult if you don't have the right players. Dansky's said they're working on tuning up the Shadowguiding so we'll have to see how it comes out.<br /> <br /> 3) Yes, while wraiths exist largely in the Underworld they have several means in interacting with the living. There are several Arcanoi, ghostly powers, that allow them to interact with the living, they can become Risen by possessing their dead bodies, there are groups and individuals that go out and seek out ghosts such as Orpheus.<br /> <br /> There's also plenty that could be done within the Underworld itself.<br /> <br /> 4) The morality is much the same as other lines. The primary enemy of WtO is Oblivion, its soldiers, the Spectres, and the wraith's Shadow. Oblivion is just that, its the force of unmaking, sitting at the very bottom of the Underworld that's slowly devouring <i>everything</i>. In Orpheus, the spiritual successor of WtO, something rises from Oblivion, or perhaps Oblivion itself awakened for the first time since the beginning of the universe. The Spectres are wraiths who have succumbed to their Shadows and Oblivion, with the Shadow becoming the wraith and the wraith's become a powerless voice in the back of their heads. They come in various forms and power levels, ranging from corrupt wraiths to creepy ghost children to ancient, inhuman king-priests of Oblivion. The Shadow is the worst parts of the human they were manifested as an independent presence within the wraith's mind, connected to Oblivion itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-04T12:39:57-08:00","id":330465,"title":"","content":"I feel like this now needs to be an annual Onyx Path forums tradition: &quot;Sell Hawthorn on X20&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi all,<br /> <br /> I also have four sorta more specific questions:<br /> <br /> 1) Just how depressing is this game? Considering I\u2019ve heard it referred to as \u201cthe Grey Book of Depression\u201d in previous conversations on this forum.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was in a fairly long Wraith game over the past two years, and I never felt depressed. But, that's me. Wraith is very open about its subjects, i.e., death and dying and regret, and that can be triggering to a lot of people. On the other hand, Wraith is also about setting your affairs in order, fixing mistakes, and maybe even moving on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) How has the whole Shadow play thing worked out for people in practice? In theory I get that it\u2019s a kinda need idea. From the anecdotes of the one group I know who have played Wraith before; it was a nightmare to actually implement.</div> </div> </div> </div>Shadow Guiding is the most controversial part of Wraith. It worked very well in my group: we all respected each other, and the ST was clear that the point of our Shadows wasn't to ruin other players; it was to provide drama and enforce theme. That worked for us, but we were all pretty upfront about what the expectations were (and we have fun when Bad Things happen to our characters). There are some groups that would be incapable of this. There are other groups that would dislike the concept in principle. Shadow Guiding ultimately comes down to taste and group cohesiveness. That said, if someone plays a Shadow, goes out of their way to make the game miserable for another player, and then declares the system unworkable, perhaps the problem is not with the game. Anything can be awful if it's not done in good faith. Shadow Guiding is about trust.<br /> <br /> Shadow Guiding <i>does </i>need some more storytelling support from the books, but that's going to be a big part of Wr20.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) How does Wraith fit into the \u201cUrban Horror/Fantasy\u201d of the WoD in regards to the fact that the majority of the game takes place in another realm removed from mortals? Something I have always liked about the WoD is the \u201cthrough a mirror darkly\u201d aspect of the setting, so I\u2019m wondering how something that steers more towards outright fantasy might work and still be part of the WoD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wraith CAN be more straight fantasy than the other games, because it mostly takes place in the Underworld. But there's a LOT of hooks in having players interact with the rest of the World of Darkness. Your most important traits are important people. places, and things from your living days. Neglect them, and Oblivion sets in. Wraith was once described to me this way: All the politics are a distraction from the truly important things, and most of those are in the mortal realm.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion#post330032\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4) What is the \u201cmorality\u201d of Wraith? Is it very \u201cshaded of moral ambiguity\u201d or more \u201cblack and white\u201d in its take on things? I usually build stories around antagonists that drive them, so I\u2019m a big believer in needing a good villain. Hence I\u2019m kinda interested in who the \u201cbad guys\u201d are in the Wraith setting (both on a minor/personal and major/existential scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>Morality is pretty baked into Wraith. You have a trait called Angst that slowly fills depending on your actions. The higher it gets, the more power your Shadow gains. It's not necessarily about being a good person; it's about finding balance with yourself. Neglect your dark side, and it'll grow resentful, and be less willing to work with you. Feed it, though...<br /> <br /> The main antagonists of the game (aside from...entropy) are Spectres. Spectres are Wraiths who've been overtaken by their Shadows. They offer a cracked mirror view of what every character could become if they're not careful.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I hope that helps. I love Wraith, and I hope you get a chance to look into it more!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-04T14:30:58-08:00","id":330539,"title":"","content":"It's a metaphor for the struggle with depression pretending to be a game about ghosts. It has the most clever mechanics of any oWoD game. It kinda saved my life. Any of these doing it for you?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T02:22:58-08:00","id":330933,"title":"","content":"From what I gather Wr20 is going to be the most radically different from its last published edition with alot of the reasons people give as it being completely unplayable being worked on. I mean there's a statement that its going to be given Horror comedy as one of the explicitly supported storytelling modes for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T03:11:10-08:00","id":330946,"title":"","content":"Wraith is by far the most rewarding rpg I have ever played, and it is also the most demanding, but I mean demanding in a good way.<br /> The world, concepts and themes of Wraith in itself requires a certain amount of maturity and communication within the gaming-group, but once you're passed that, there really is no limit to what you can do with it.<br /> Of course, it sets the stage for some deep, psychodramatic horror roleplaying, but since the Underworld is so diverse and basically houses any imaginative soul that wasn't able to move on after death, the realms collectively called the Far Shores open up for vast amounts of story possibillities. Perhaps Tolkien whent there after he passed away and finished building what he started...and then succumbed to his Shadow, went completely mad and took the guise of Sauron himself.<br /> Such a story would of course be very far from the basic concepts of Wraith, but still possible within the framework of RAW.<br /> Shadowguiding was never a problem for my group, but also different for each player and what they were comfortable with. We started simple, using only the mechanics and very little roleplaying for the shadow, but soon everyone got into it and wanted to act in character more and more, but still respecting the story and other players.<br /> <br /> Just some thoughts for now, I'm sure I'll write some more later <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Oh, and this is my first post on the forum...yayyy!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4427-malfean_sleepover","name":"Malfean_Sleepover","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4427_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T03:57:56-08:00","id":330972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=330465#post330465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel like this now needs to be an annual Onyx Path forums tradition: &quot;Sell Hawthorn on X20&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>As much as I would like to become an Onyx Path tradition, I'm afraid the stars are not right for that. <br /> <br /> I'm not a fan of Changeling the Dreaming or Hunter the Reckoning. So those two games are right out from the start.<br /> <br /> While I do not mind Mummy the Resurrection or Demon the Fallen, I find their NWoD counterparts (Mummy the Curse and Demon the Descent) far more to my taste. To the point were I can't see myself owning them.<br /> <br /> I already have a keen like for Kindred of the East, so I will not need convincing to pledge for that.<br /> <br /> So alas Yossarian, this is the last time I will be posting for a &quot;Sell Me&quot; reason (at least for the X20 books). <br /> <br /> Anyway, back to the topic at hand...<br /> <br /> I am finding peoples responses helpful and some of the accounts given are very interesting to say the least (atamajakki's stands out for sure). So thanks for replying and engaging in conversation about Wraith. Sinking my teeth into the Wiki a little more as well. Liking the Neverborn and Grandma as big bads for the setting.<br /> <br /> Let discussion continue!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T06:14:35-08:00","id":331016,"title":"","content":"I have played a whole campaign of wraith and it has been one of the best rpg experiences that I ever had. Wraith is a very sad and bleak game if you only see the surface of it, however deep down wraith is game about hope, redemption and growth. No matter how bleak the situation or how strong your suffering is, there is always a light at the end of the tunnel. Giving yourself to oblivion is the easy path. Fighting to keep going on, to care, to try and find meaning in your existence is difficult and painful but so much more rewarding. Wraith taught me a valuable lesson when I was at my lowest: to never give up. What we do, what we feel, what we care about, everything matters and that is what makes living worth it. Because at the end of the day you can look at yourself in the mirror and say I'm still here and I'm still fighting and that is the greatest victory of them all.<br /> However Wraith has also been one of the most infuriating and frustrating experiences I ever had. Wraith is a rpg that demands a lot from the players both in terms of implication and maturity. Perhaps I just had bad luck but man, do I have some horror stories about how people behave playing this game. It seems that emotional maturity is severally lacking with some people. The shadow system although a great idea on paper is almost completely unplayable in practice. People just take it too personally or they don't take it seriously at all. At the beginning I started playing wraith only with my best friend and the gm. The gm carried our shadows the first sessions and then he let us give it a try, we had a great time. Then more people joined and i didn't end well, at all. <br /> My advice is that wraith is totally worth playing it but make it sure that you are with people you trust and can get intimate with, playing it with strangers or people you don't really trust is a recipe for disaster. I hope my advice helps you. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2964-firanai","name":"Firanai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2964_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T08:47:22-08:00","id":331160,"title":"","content":"A few thoughts I have on <b>Wraith</b>:<br /> <br /> If you're familiar with the NWOD, I've always thought that <b>Promethean </b>was the more accurate successor to <b>Wraith </b>than <b>Geist</b>. The themes and moods of both games focus more on humanity and what it means to be a person. They're coming at it from opposite ends, though. <b>Promethean </b>builds up to having a human life, <b>Wraith </b>looks back on what that life was and the regrets and losses.<br /> <br /> <b>Wraith</b> requires an examined life. The Passions and Fetters and Shadow are all based around what the character was like in life. What they miss, what they treasured, what they regretted, what would be their inner-most asshole, how they died, who they were, who they wanted to be, who was important to them, and what are they going to do now that they are in this place.<br /> <br /> <b>Wraith </b>is the most alien environment because there is no basis in anything familiar. The Umbra can be strange, but much of it is based on the natural world, or a Mage has created a world based around their ideas, or there's a theme within this random Realm that has occurred. Stygia is based on what people left behind, molded and mutilated to fit their needs at the time, stretched out over the choices of millions of Wraiths over thousands of years. It's a modern high rise built on a public courthouse built on a Gothic church built on a Greek temple built on an Egyptian pyramid. There is no cohesion beyond what was needed at the time to survive. There are no themes. The physics that would break these buildings in the Skinlands does not apply here. And that's just Stygia, where there has been an ATTEMPT at cohesion and a single guiding vision. The Far Shores can be even worse.<br /> <br /> And then there's The Tempest. A literal Sea of Madness. A place that erodes the soul, tears away identity and twists all of it towards entropy. Before at least the minds of humanity, many and shifting though they may be, could be attributed to the environment. Within the Tempest, things that were never human helped build that place. Things that have been so twisted as to no longer BE human are roaming the place. Things that bear no relation to reality, humanity, logic, physics or anything else wander the Tempest for purposes only they know.....maybe.<br /> <br /> All of that together makes it a very evocative environment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T09:17:39-08:00","id":331191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malfean_Sleepover</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=330946#post330946\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. Perhaps Tolkien whent there after he passed away and finished building what he started...and then succumbed to his Shadow, went completely mad and took the guise of Sauron himself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or Tolkien became a Ferryman and Sauron is his Pasiphae....<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T09:24:34-08:00","id":331197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=330972#post330972\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As much as I would like to become an Onyx Path tradition, I'm afraid the stars are not right for that.<br /> <br /> I'm not a fan of Changeling the Dreaming or Hunter the Reckoning. So those two games are right out from the start.<br /> <br /> While I do not mind Mummy the Resurrection or Demon the Fallen, I find their NWoD counterparts (Mummy the Curse and Demon the Descent) far more to my taste. To the point were I can't see myself owning them.<br /> <br /> I already have a keen like for Kindred of the East, so I will not need convincing to pledge for that.<br /> <br /> So alas Yossarian, this is the last time I will be posting for a &quot;Sell Me&quot; reason (at least for the X20 books).<br /> <br /> Anyway, back to the topic at hand...<br /> <br /> I am finding peoples responses helpful and some of the accounts given are very interesting to say the least (atamajakki's stands out for sure). So thanks for replying and engaging in conversation about Wraith. Sinking my teeth into the Wiki a little more as well. Liking the Neverborn and Grandma as big bads for the setting.<br /> <br /> Let discussion continue!</div> </div> </div> </div>Why not have it continue? What if you could be convinced to give them a try? <br /> <br /> I have dabbled in Changeling (ran one short chronicle and little bits here and there) and, overall, like the NWOD version better, but I would definitely like to see animated discussion about it.<br /> <br /> I prepped and started a Reckoning chronicle. It really looked like it could end up going somewhere interesting. Some dynamics in the gaming group ended up shifting us to another Mage game (our default), but when I read through my notes I see the potential (and wonder what demons were lurking in my brain to set something that horrific up). I'm not sure we'll see it in any case. I haven't seen the level of rabid fandom for it as I have for VtM, WtR, MtA and WrtO.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T09:41:24-08:00","id":331213,"title":"","content":"I love Wraith. I used to call it &quot;the best game I've never played.&quot; But since then, I have both played and run it. Including playing in a Dark Kingdom of Wire game at Gen Con back in the day. It doesn't get more bleak and depressing than that. Though there were several people there with the usual Wraith issues, the few of us who were passionate about it, made it an awesome experience. And the Shadowguiding rules worked well enough, even in a game of 12 strangers. <br /> <br /> I've run one chronicle and one &quot;mini-chronicle&quot; where the players took a break from their mages and played wraiths in the same setting who were playing a story tangential but related to the main Mage chronicle plot (and one that illuminated some underpinnings to that game's plot arcs). Neither one of those dealt with a lot of the &quot;coolness potential&quot; of Wraith settings. Both were set in necropoli. No excursions to the Labyrinth or Stygia or the Far Shores or other Dark Kingdoms. Still, both games were amazing. Once again, the Shadowguiding and Harrowing rules seemed to work fine for us. That may have had something to do with the players being used to taking on other roles from time to time during the other characters' Seekings in Mage. (Which, when I think about it, I tend to run as sort of 'reverse Harrowings). (Sytgian politics was off stage to a degree in both games, but not front and center... I think they met one Wraith messenger who was one Fetter away from not being able to come that close to the Skinlands anymore). I did use the Ferrymen to a degree. But they really showed up more in the Mage game than the Wraith game. (One was set in St. Louis and the rivers were very important thematically and plot-wise. A Ferryman in a ghostly steamship who would take people where they needed to go - not wanted - for the price they needed to pay, was just too good to pass up). <br /> <br /> One thing I did notice about a necropolis based game, all the historical research I poured into it paid off in spades. Finding appropriate leaders and notables, knowing what Memorium was present around the city, figuring out what wraiths and sorts of dead folks would gravitate to the Hierarchy, Renegades or Heretics (or just lay low and try to stay out of the way... which was usually a sort of default Hierrachy position), etc etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T10:02:07-08:00","id":331246,"title":"","content":"Just a note, I suggest this thread be moved to the brand new <b>Wraith</b> forum board!<br /> <br /> Oh, by the bye, there's a brand new <b>Wraith</b> forum board!<br /> <br /> <b>Edit</b>: And there it happened! Looks like your sweet <b><span style=\"color:#008080\">Phaolan</span></b> was just a little ahead of the mods... but just a wee bit!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":409,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","user":"Phaolan","date":". 12-05-2014, 01:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-05T19:49:49-08:00","id":331766,"title":"","content":"Oh I wouldn't worry about C20. If Hawthorn doesn't set one up for Changeling I will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T06:29:48-08:00","id":332175,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=331766#post331766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh I wouldn't worry about C20. If Hawthorn doesn't set one up for Changeling I will.</div> </div> </div> </div>Its not an easy weight to carry AkatsukiLeader, having your fellow forumites spill on what makes a game awesome and what might give you issues if you try and play/run it. Its a heavy burden, but I believe in you. So the baton can be passed for C20. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Actually Ajax brought up a point I kinda missed in my original post:<br /> <br /> How many different kinds of game can you play in the Wraith setting?<br /> <br /> Obviously their is the core difference between Underworld vs Skinlands stuff. However, I'm wondering about all the different game types you can squeeze out within the Underworld.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T06:56:30-08:00","id":332182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=332175#post332175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How many different kinds of game can you play in the Wraith setting?<br /> <br /> Obviously their is the core difference between Underworld vs Skinlands stuff. However, I'm wondering about all the different game types you can squeeze out within the Underworld.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, theres so much potential! Off the top of my head:<br /> <br /> Deep and personal, revolving around resolving Passions and Fetters.<br /> Politics in Stygia among the elders of the underworld.<br /> Survival in the Shadowlands from slavers, spectres and other threats.<br /> Exploring the Tempest, encountering all kinds of weird.<br /> Doomslaying in the Labyrinth, the Wraith equivalent to a dungeon crawl.<br /> Spectres! Play the villains of the underworld!<br /> Travelling to other Dark Kingdoms.<br /> Playing as wraiths from other Dark Kingdoms (this is a very broad category, depending on which Dark Kingdom you play in).<br /> High epics, looking for Charon and following the meta-plot.<br /> Guildwraiths, hiding from the Hierarchy and plotting their return. This can be very varied depending on the Guild or Guilds.<br /> Religious and philosophical game with the Heretics as protagonists.<br /> Crossover with one or more other cWoD lines. Mage and Vampire are especially suited for this.<br /> Day to day Shadowlands stuff, as low-ranking members of the Hierarchy.<br /> War! Either the Great War or the Stygia/Jade conflict.<br /> <br /> There posibilities are literally endless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4449-eisenkreutzer","name":"EisenKreutzer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4449_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T07:09:31-08:00","id":332186,"title":"","content":"Haunting the Quick the hell away from your Haunt/Fetters. Outrage, Pandemonium, and Inhabit are wonderful for this.<br /> Mob stories. The Spooks, thanks to their Guildbook, are excellent for this.<br /> Stories of the entertainment industry, with the Sandmen and Chanteurs particularly standing out here.<br /> Espionage. Infiltrate the Hierarchy/Renegades/Heretics/Guilds/Dark Kingdoms/Spectres etc.<br /> The pursuit of Transcendence.<br /> The pursuit of Redemption, if you're a Spectre.<br /> Psychodramas.<br /> Romance stories.<br /> Want to go medical? The Wraith20 Usurers offer you the chance to become a wraithly doctor, investigating the strange afflictions of the Underworld. Prefer to deal with more psychological issues? How about the Pardoners?<br /> Good old-fashioned ghostly revenge stories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-06T09:09:35-08:00","id":332236,"title":"","content":"Yeah there's ton of different ways to play WtO. You can run the deeply personal games involving the PCs trying to pursuit Transcendence to dealing with pesky ghost-hunters messing with your Haunt to dungeon-crawls in the Labyrinth to adventures on the Midnight Express or in the other Dark Kingdoms to political games of the Hierarchy and the Legions to all out war as seen in the Great War and everything in between.<br /> <br /> Like all the other lines, there's a lot to explore with WtO. And I'd also add to this WtO's spiritual successor, Orpheus. Even if they weren't adding it to Wr20, the Orpheus line has a great deal of material to farm ideas from for WtO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-08T10:58:18-08:00","id":334346,"title":"","content":"So as another couple of questions that spring up from my casual reading of on-line sources about Wraith.<br /> <br /> 1) If you are a Wraith that finds itself in the Skinlands in an immaterial state, how easy is it for you to interact with the material world? <br /> <br /> To expand on this a bit, is it made easier by the intervention/involvement of other supernaturals? Can Mages with Spirit and Vampires with Necromancy maybe help you out? Just thinking of possible points of &quot;cross-over&quot; that might facilitate both PC and NPC actions and plots. <br /> <br /> 2) How jarring is the transition of game themes and type if you start off playing Wraith very much involved with the Skinland stuff and then get sucked down into the Underworld? Does it really, really alter the game? Is the tonal/thematic change kinda jarring?<br /> <br /> Going form an urban modern horror game to a sorta dark fantasy setting and all. A lot of people I know like to roleplay in the WoD precisely because its urban and modern and just basically our world. So their is no forced parlance or anything to dilute a player/characters interaction and reaction to the world. I' kinda wondering how Wraith might get around that while still involving Underworld stuff. <br /> <br /> 3) What's up with the Neverborn and Grandma?<br /> <br /> The latter seems to have been added in Orpheus for the purposes of providing an even bigger &quot;big bad&quot; above the Neverborn. Is she suppose to be their mum or something? Does anyone know how she might be setting worked in Wr20? <br /> <br /> The former seem interesting, however, if their all &quot;dead but dreaming&quot; (I get a serious Lovecraft vibe of these guys, in a good way) in the Wraith setting how much impact do they have on said setting? How can they impact the world short of waking up and causing obvious doomsday plot? <br /> <br /> 4) So Wraiths from other Dark Kingdoms have different souls based on cultural beliefs basically? <br /> <br /> Does this also tie into the fact they get different ghostly powers? I noticed the 185K stretch goal talks about the Jade kingdom getting different powers. Are they alternate levels of the same powers or are they brand new power types?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-08T11:30:37-08:00","id":334390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=334346#post334346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So as another couple of questions that spring up from my casual reading of on-line sources about Wraith.<br /> <br /> 1) If you are a Wraith that finds itself in the Skinlands in an immaterial state, how easy is it for you to interact with the material world?<br /> <br /> To expand on this a bit, is it made easier by the intervention/involvement of other supernaturals? Can Mages with Spirit and Vampires with Necromancy maybe help you out? Just thinking of possible points of &quot;cross-over&quot; that might facilitate both PC and NPC actions and plots.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not exactly sure what you are asking here, in the first half of the question. The Shadowlands and the Skinlands are like reflections of one another, so in a sense wraiths are always in the Skinlands in an immaterial state. A wraith can see and hear anything that occurs in the Skinlands, but remains invisible and intangible to mortals and other SKinlands creatures (unless some magic is involved).<br /> <br /> Any wraith can pass through solid Skinlands objects by spending a point of Corpus, passing through doors, walls and even people with minimal effort and discomfort.<br /> Actually manifesting in the SKinlands is tougher, though, and requires Arkanoi. The Outrage, Inhabit, Puppetry and Embody Arkanoi enable wraiths to affect the Skinlands in different ways. Pandemonium and Flux also allows a wraith to affect the Skinlands.<br /> With Embody, a wraith can push themselves through the Shroud and manifest fully in the Skinlands. Inhabit and Puppetry allow a wraith to posess objects or people respectively, while Outrage allows a wraith to manipulate objects in the Skinlands crudely.<br /> <br /> As for other supernaturals, it varies. There is little a mage cannot accomplish with enough preparation and knowledge, and allowing wraiths to manifest in the Skinlands is certainly within their power. There is also a Necromancy Ritual that lowers the Shroud, which can help wraiths with the correct Arkanoi to manifest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) How jarring is the transition of game themes and type if you start off playing Wraith very much involved with the Skinland stuff and then get sucked down into the Underworld? Does it really, really alter the game? Is the tonal/thematic change kinda jarring?<br /> <br /> Going form an urban modern horror game to a sorta dark fantasy setting and all. A lot of people I know like to roleplay in the WoD precisely because its urban and modern and just basically our world. So their is no forced parlance or anything to dilute a player/characters interaction and reaction to the world. I' kinda wondering how Wraith might get around that while still involving Underworld stuff. </div> </div> </div>The transition doesn't need to be jarring at all. It can be as simple as a shift in focus, like an evolving Tv show. Look at the show SUpernatural, for example, and how it slowly grows in scope; from a show about two brothers hunting ghosts, it slowly grows into an apocalyptic showdown between heaven and hell. A Wraith chronicle can grow in the same way, from close and personal to broad and sweeping, with higher and higher stakes as time moves on.<br /> <br /> Urban horror elements don't have to go away entirely. Since the Shadowlands mirror the Skinlands almost exactly, you will always be wandering the streets of New York (as an example), even though the city will look decaying and rotten, and there may be some details which remind you that you are in the Shadowlands (such as destroyed buildings that still stand, and wraithly alterations to the skyline built with soulsteel).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) What's up with the Neverborn and Grandma? <br /> <br /> The latter seems to have been added in Orpheus for the purposes of providing an even bigger &quot;big bad&quot; above the Neverborn. Is she suppose to be their mum or something? Does anyone know how she might be setting worked in Wr20?<br /> <br /> The former seem interesting, however, if their all &quot;dead but dreaming&quot; (I get a serious Lovecraft vibe of these guys, in a good way) in the Wraith setting how much impact do they have on said setting? How can they impact the world short of waking up and causing obvious doomsday plot? </div> </div> </div>I haven't read the entire Orpheus line, so I don't know much about Grandma, but I can tell you about the Neverborn. The Neverborn are Lovecraftian entities who arose from the darkness before the world was created, and who dwell sleeping at the edge of Oblivion deep within the Labyrinth.<br /> Though they slumber, they still make their will known to their spectre priests and attendants, who obey their dreaming commands. As antagonists they serve the purpose of dreadful presences behind the scenes, who combat existence through their spectral servants. All the advice in the books says to keep them behind the scnes, and I think thats good advice. You don't kill Cthulhu in a Call of Cthulhu campaign, and the same holds true for the Neverborn.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4) So Wraiths from other Dark Kingdoms have different souls based on cultural beliefs basically?<br /> <br /> Does this also tie into the fact they get different ghostly powers? I noticed the 185K stretch goal talks about the Jade kingdom getting different powers. Are they alternate levels of the same powers or are they brand new power types? </div> </div> </div>Wraiths are wraiths no matter what Dark Kingdom they come from. The Jade Empire wraiths aren't different from Stygian wraiths the way Kuei-Jin are from Kindred. They do have access to unique Arkanoi, though, which have arisen due to special circumstances in their culture and history, which are rare or unheard of in Stygia. The Jade Empire has three or four (can't remember right now) unique Arkanoi that reflect the cultural differences between the Jade Empire and Stygia in terms of how thei view and treat their Shadows, Spectres and soulforging and soulsteel.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4449-eisenkreutzer","name":"EisenKreutzer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4449_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-08T12:03:30-08:00","id":334406,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EisenKreutzer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=334390#post334390\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wraiths are wraiths no matter what Dark Kingdom they come from. The Jade Empire wraiths aren't different from Stygian wraiths the way Kuei-Jin are from Kindred. They do have access to unique Arkanoi, though, which have arisen due to special circumstances in their culture and history, which are rare or unheard of in Stygia. The Jade Empire has three or four (can't remember right now) unique Arkanoi that reflect the cultural differences between the Jade Empire and Stygia in terms of how thei view and treat their Shadows, Spectres and soulforging and soulsteel.</div> </div> </div> </div>Five: the Way of the Artisan, the Way of the Scholar, the Way of the Merchant, the Way of the Farmer, and the Way of the Soul.<br /> <br /> So, the answer to the original question is yes, according to Wraith: The Great War, which posits that access to certain Arcanoi of a Dark Kingdom is unique to wraiths of that Dark Kingdom, based on their particular soul configuration (the Ways of Jade, Swar's Displace, and the Bush of Ghosts' Behest) - and they risk losing access to those powers the longer they stay in another Dark Kingdom, as they come to identify with its soul configuration.<br /> <br /> (However, it should be noted that Doomslayers holds that any wraith can learn any Arcanos<i>.)</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-08T12:14:18-08:00","id":334409,"title":"","content":"You forgot the Arcanoi, Chains of the Emperor. It's the rarest of the Dark Kingdom of Jade Arcanoi, exclusive to the Emperor and his Immortal Guard but its still an Arcanoi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-09T04:23:36-08:00","id":335026,"title":"","content":"So do the Dark Kingdom of Jade Arcanoi do notably different things from any of the default ones? Are they simple &quot;alternate&quot; takes on the same kinda powers?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-09T05:43:23-08:00","id":335062,"title":"","content":"No, they are unique Arcanoi to the Dark Kingdom of Jade. They do have some similarities with the Western Arcanoi but are unique in their own right. And the wraiths of the DKoJ can learn all the Western Arcanoi save Castigate, though both Inhabit and Usury are quite rare.<br /> <br /> Similarly other Dark Kingdoms have unique Arcanoi. The Swar has Displace, the Dark Kingdom of Ivory has Behest and the wraiths of Les Invisibles has Connaissance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-09T05:45:37-08:00","id":335065,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=335062#post335062\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Similarly other Dark Kingdoms have unique Arcanoi. The Swar has Displace, the Dark Kingdom of Ivory has Behest and the wraiths of Les Invisibles has Connaissance.</div> </div> </div> </div>Connaissance is an exception to the whole unique Arcanoi thing, in that there are ways for non-Caribbean wraiths to learn it (mostly through making a pact with the Shadow). That may be modified if it's ever updated to Wr20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":51,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","user":"marin","date":". 12-09-2014, 09:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-09T06:06:59-08:00","id":335079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=334346#post334346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) What's up with the Neverborn and Grandma?<br /> <br /> The latter seems to have been added in Orpheus for the purposes of providing an even bigger &quot;big bad&quot; above the Neverborn. Is she suppose to be their mum or something? Does anyone know how she might be setting worked in Wr20?</div> </div> </div> </div>Effectively, yes, Grandmother is the Neverborn's &quot;mother&quot;, insofar as they have a parent - in creating them, she created new ways to not exist. What <i>exactly</i> she is is left open to the needs of a chronicle.<br /> <br /> She may well not be addressed in Wr20, as it took the Sixth Great Maelstrom to wake her up, and Wr20 is set at a point before that. If she is, it'd likely be as a rumour or something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T06:15:16-08:00","id":340782,"title":"","content":"OK so I've been away a while with stuff(tm) but I'm back now.<br /> <br /> Want to thank everyone for the answers they are providing me here. I've got a few more questions lined up:<br /> <br /> 1) Has anyone following this thread set a Wraith game in another Dark Kingdom? How did this different setting for the game influence the feel of Wraith in general? Was their enough detail about the Kingdom in question for you to run such a game with minimal &quot;bumps&quot;? <br /> <br /> 2) Is a &quot;multi-Kingdom&quot; type player troupe viable for a Wraith game? If so how unusually/special-snowflake like is it? What sort of Wraith stories can be told by a multi-Kingdom game that might not be possible in a straight Stygia game? <br /> <br /> 3) Can someone give a players point of view on what a Dark Arcanoi is? How does possessing such a power alter the Wraith? What is the cost? What's the social sigma attached to knowing such powers? <br /> <br /> If anyone needs me to provide more context for these questions I'll be happy to provide such.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T06:52:40-08:00","id":340798,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=340782#post340782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) Is a &quot;multi-Kingdom&quot; type player troupe viable for a Wraith game? If so how unusually/special-snowflake like is it? What sort of Wraith stories can be told by a multi-Kingdom game that might not be possible in a straight Stygia game?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an option in Wraith: the Great War, in the context of wraiths who'd normally fall under other Dark Kingdoms ending up in Stygian territory thanks to the First World War. You have the Guilds hunting down wraiths from other Kingdoms for the secrets of their Arcanoi (because they're unaware that learning such Arcanoi is based on soul structure), and many of the wraiths in question trying to find a way back home through the Fourth Great Maelstrom.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=340782#post340782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) Can someone give a players point of view on what a Dark Arcanoi is? How does possessing such a power alter the Wraith? What is the cost? What's the social sigma attached to knowing such powers?<br /> <br /> If anyone needs me to provide more context for these questions I'll be happy to provide such.</div> </div> </div> </div>All of the following comes from Doomslayers:<br /> <br /> Normally, Dark Arcanoi are available only to Spectres, but Doomslayers can learn them thanks to their exposure to Spectres and Oblivion, which means any of: a Pact of Doom with the Shadow, learning from a Spectre who knows them, learning from a wraith who knows them, or implants. Learning them from a wraith or Spectre takes on the order of months.<br /> <br /> They cost basically twice as much as do standard Arcanoi, and, if bought during character creation, can only be bought with freebie points.<br /> <br /> Wraiths switch out the Angst and Being costs of Dark Arcanoi for Pathos and Willpower. All dice rolled are considered Shadow Dice (that is, each 1 rolled strengthens the Shadow).<br /> <br /> The Doomslayers want to be very, very sure you know what you're doing with Dark Arcanoi; it requires approval from the higher-up Doomslayers in order to start teaching or learning them. Teaching without authorisation means the Hierarchy and Doomslayers are going to come down on you like a ton of soulsteel bricks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":51,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","user":"marin","date":". 12-15-2014, 09:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T07:44:25-08:00","id":340813,"title":"","content":"Only the Dark Kingdoms of Jade and Wire got their own books, the rest are talked about in the Player's Guide. So those two Dark Kingdoms are fairly playable while the others there is enough to play wraiths of those Kingdoms but no quite so much in those Dark Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> But yeah the best way to play a multi-Kingdom wraith game is in Wraith: the Great War due to the setting of World War One. It is also one of the best books in WtO and one of my absolute favorite books. I'd actually recommend picking it up now as something to tide people over until Wr20 comes out<i>.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T07:50:53-08:00","id":340815,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=340813#post340813\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Only the Dark Kingdoms of Jade and Wire got their own books, the rest are talked about in the Player's Guide. So those two Dark Kingdoms are fairly playable while the others there is enough to play wraiths of those Kingdoms but no quite so much in those Dark Kingdoms.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also a fair chunk in the Shadow Player's Guide, discussing how the wraiths of each Kingdom see their dark sides.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-22T11:46:41-08:00","id":347566,"title":"","content":"OK, so I'm back again. As I am sure people can understand, this time of year keeps me away from the forums for days at a time.<br /> <br /> So having read up some more stuff I have two additional questions:<br /> <br /> 1) What's up with the Ferrymen? <br /> <br /> Sorry I'll rephrase that...<br /> <br /> 1) How have people used the Ferrymen/Parisphae in their chronicles? <br /> 1.1) How can the Ferrymen/Parisphae be used in unconventional ways in a Wraith Chronicle?<br /> <br /> To clarify, what I mean by unconventional because I can see how they could be used as sorta &quot;plot giving NPC's&quot; (which is how most accounts I have found on them in online chronicles use them). I mean has anyone used a Ferryman as a antagonist for mysterious reasons? Heck, has anyone ran a Chronicle were some of the PC's ended up as Ferrymen (is such a game even viable? Or is it like being Methuselah's in Vampire?) <br /> <br /> Also, I find their fluff a little...odd. I mean its implied that as a power group they pretty much have no equals save the Neverborn and their higher-ranking servants. The whole thing with the Dark Kingdom of Wire implies they could pretty much decapitate the Stygian government and remake Stygia into a much nicer place, but they don't for reasons I have not gathered yet.<br /> <br /> Also they seem rather tied up with the OWoD Mummy stuff, with Anubis being a bit part of their backstory. I don't really now how much other Mummy stuff bleeds through into Wraith, but could you run a Chronicle involving Ferrymen as a focus and not have them ultimately owe their uber-wraith state to another game lines major NPC? <br /> <br /> I'm also find interested in the Parisphae, they seem to be major Oblivion orientated bad-guys that scare the crap out of pretty much everything in the Labyrinth save the Neverborn. However, despite being &quot;the Generals of Oblivion&quot; they don't strike me as the sort to be leading massive hoards of Specters. They seem to be a more mysterious and insidious evil then that. So I was wondering if anyone had used them as notable antagonists (and how they were used) and if anyone has used one as a &quot;big bad&quot; for a Wraith game before. <br /> <br /> Also, do they really just spend most of their time &quot;undoing the good the Ferrymen do&quot;? That seems like a bit of a waste to me, I understand the thematic symbolism there and the idea that even Ferryman have a darkside, its just that its manifest and free-roaming as opposed to internal and corruptive. I'm not saying I can't see a lot of good stories about a Ferryman its Parisphae playing plot-speed-chess against one another with the PC's in the middle (hell that' an appealing idea for me). I am just wondering if people have used Parisphae removed from that particular thematic crux that they obviously inhabit well.<br /> <br /> 2) What is up with the Far Shores?<br /> <br /> Sorry, sorry, happened again. Take two<br /> <br /> 2) How have people used the Far Shores in their Wraith Chronicles? <br /> <br /> I'm kinda curious about the Far Shores, very little seems to be written on them. They are involved with Transcendence but going there does not give you any extra protection from your Specter it seems. After all even the Shining Ones largely became Shadow Eaten over time. The fact they are left so vague suggests to me that they are kinda purposefully left as a part of the setting the ST can customize and make into their own thing. So I'm wondering at all the different ways people have personalized them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T19:32:16-08:00","id":348720,"title":"","content":"1) You have to be careful with the Ferrymen (and to a lesser extent the Pasiphae) as they are pretty damn powerful and can easily hog the spotlight from the PCs. Now I've never played WtO, trying to run a PbP WtGW game here on the forums at the moment, but I have read some of the pre-made chronicles that do feature a Ferrymen.<br /> <br /> My suggestions are the following based on what I've read and my own thoughts:<br /> <br /> -Less is better. Both the Ferrymen and the Pasiphae are very mysterious beings, seldom seen by the common wraith. Plus the Pasiphae rarely leave the Labyrinth. So use them sparely, showing up every once and awhile but not so much that they start to lose their enigmatic nature.<br /> <br /> -A savior. Some times the PCs end up biting off more than they can chew it looks like the whole Circle's going to be wiped out. Having a Ferryman showing up at the least second to save them all and drive back whatever is trying to kill them.<br /> <br /> -A fond of knowledge. Some Ferrymen are old, quite old. They know things that most wraiths haven't even heard of. Naturally they can be very useful for providing PCs with knowledge they can't find elsewhere. Hell simply finding them could be a mini-quest of itself.<br /> <br /> -Transportation. The Ferrymen are...well Ferrymen. They control the Midnight Express, perhaps the most reliable means of transportation in the Underworld. Some times your PCs need to get somewhere, somewhere far or hard to get to. Getting onto the Midnight Express or finding and convincing a Ferrymen to help out, especially if they're attempting to pursuit Transcendence.<br /> <br /> -A potential recruit. Perhaps one of the PCs is unknowingly being considered as a potential Ferrymen and is being watched from afar by one of them. Maybe every once and awhile appearing to lend some help for seemingly no reason whatsoever. Don't tell anything to your players and let them stew on why a Ferrymen seems to be showing up periodically.<br /> <br /> 2) I've never really touched the Far Shores. Honestly they've always felt a bit like a distraction in the modern setting. If they ever did a Dark Ages: Wraith than I could see the appear of them but as it stands they just don't interest me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-26T18:49:18-08:00","id":351167,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\"><u><b>Ferrymen and Pasiphae</b></u><br /> A long time ago, there was this delightfully brilliant RPG called H.o.L. When I discovered the game, it was being published under the Black Dog label. In this game, there was a badass gun-slinging NPC named \u201cEdgar Sparingly.\u201d His purpose was to give Storytellers (Holmeisters) a tool to bail out characters if they did something stupid. The final advice given on this character, iirc, was to use him, \u201c\u2026sparingly.\u201d<br /> <br /> That was my take on Ferrymen. During the times I found myself as ST, I had a Ferryman NPC on stand-by, written in the same spirit as Edgar. Just in case the troupe did something stupid (or \u2013 and I\u2019ll never admit this to them \u2013 in case I inadvertently set them up for failure). <br /> <br /> I never personally used the Pasiphae, but our regular ST used one for a story arc involving my character and his shadow. It added a little <i>je ne sais quoi</i> to the story; more so than just a Nephwrack would. <br /> <br /> [My observation: Pasiphae can be played outside the norm of most Nephwracks, but below the power curve of Never/Once-born. They work well for when you need an *evil* antagonist without all the baggage of the culty Nephwracks and their hordes (even Coldheart had his cadre).]<br /> <br /> In either case, give them the Arcanoi <i>Plot Device</i> OOOOO.<br /> <br /> Insofar as the Mummy stuff and the Ritual of Severance (? \u2013 been awhile since EoE), it doesn\u2019t come up unless you want it to. If you don\u2019t like the idea of it being dependent on some Mummy fluff, make up an uber-Castigate Art. <br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>The Far Shores</b></u><br /> These are McGuffins just waiting to happen. Need a backdrop that doesn\u2019t really fit anywhere else in the Underworld? Make it a Far Shore.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">A cabal of Knights Templars that created a Valhalla-esque \u201cafterlife\u201d devoted to accumulating Relics of war in order to achieve Transcendence by becoming [martially] \u201cwithout equal\u201d?</span><br /> <br /> Far Shore.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">A hub for aerial pirates (a la <i>Talespin</i>) that work with the Renegades to accrue wealth, thereby achieving the necessary funds to enter Heaven?</span><br /> <br /> Far Shore.<br /> <br /> I took the fact that there\u2019s so little written on them as a license to do whatever I needed them to do. YMMV.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T02:30:25-08:00","id":358163,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:12px\">Hmm . . . I think a quick explanation of Wraith's cosmology would help a lot here. You're familiar with <b>Werewolf </b>and <b>Mage</b>, right? If so, that'll help a lot.<br /> <br /> Wraiths exist in the Underworld, what mages know as the Low Umbra - the dark twin of the Umbra that werewolves visit. The Kuei-jin know it as the Yin World. Playing off the usual metaphor, you can think of the Underworld as multiple sub-floors of a building.<br /> <br /> The Shadowlands are the ground floor. Separated from the Skinlands (the mundane/living world) by a metaphysical barrier known as the Shroud (the Underworld's equivalent to the Wall), it is very, very similar to the Skinlands . . . but on their side of the Shroud, the buildings are rotting and decaying, a grim parody of what the living world looks like. But the landmarks are the same, and a map of the living world applies to the Shadowlands, too.<br /> <br /> But the Shroud is so very much more porous than the Wall - there's a reason they use a cloth metaphor. Even a newly dead wraith can look through the Shroud to see their loved ones, and even many completely mundane humans can glimpse wraiths out of the corner of their eye, or just barely sense their icy touch (&quot;feels like someone walked over my grave&quot;).<br /> <br /> However, not every wraith can reach the Shadowlands - they have to use their fetters (emotionally important people/objects/places) to pull themselves that close to the realm of the living. That's rarely a problem for recently dead wraiths, but older ones . . .well, things get destroyed, people die, etc. In fact, destroying a wraith's fetters is a popular battle strategy, because it cuts them off from the Shadowlands and many of their passions (unfinished business).<br /> <br /> But there are &quot;basements&quot; below the Shadowlands. Places where the rules are different. Some wraiths can travel freely between &quot;levels&quot;, others have to rely on Nihils - strange tears and holes that occasionally appear in the fabric of the Underworld. Some are as regular as Old Faithful in Yellowstone, others are more random.<br /> <br /> Below the Shadowlands lie the Sunless Sea - a storm wracked sea that contains the Tempest, the Dark Kingdoms (such as Stygia), and the Far Shores. Most newly dead wraiths can't explore this level much - the same fetters that bind them to existence chain them to the nearest Dark Kingdom and the surrounding Tempest. For a Western (American, European, etc.) wraith, that would be Stygia.<br /> <br /> A Dark Kingdom is built of the &quot;ghosts&quot; of what the living have treasured and valued. Churches, palaces . . . Stygia, the Dark Kingdom of Iron, even contains an intact version of the Great Library of Alexandria. Which makes sense - Stygia was built by Western wraiths, for Western wraiths.<br /> <br /> A massive megalopolis, built across seven &quot;islands&quot; in the Sunless Sea, some parts of Stygia are gorgeous. A wraith who's into history can spend months just touring the nicer parts, marveling at architectural wonders long lost to the living world. All the lost treasures of antiquity are there.<br /> <br /> Other parts of the city are . . .less pleasant. Stygia was never supposed to be this big - it was just supposed to be a stopping point on the quest for Transcendence (which the mages call Ascension and the kuei-jin call &quot;dah&quot;). However, many wraiths have lost faith in the possibility of Transcendence, and are content to stay in Stygia. And the living can only build (and destroy) wondrous palaces so fast. . .<br /> <br /> The result has been the explosive growth of massive slums . . . and the unending screams from the soul forges. If the living won't provide building materials, well . . . there's only one other source of raw material. And that's other wraiths.<br /> <br /> Technically, only slaves and convicted felons are supposed to be melted down into soulsteel. But its an open secret that people do go &quot;missing&quot; in the slums, and only a fool walks around the bad parts of town unarmed . . .<br /> <br /> As for the other Dark Kingdoms or the Far Shores . . .well, most wraiths know they exist. But getting there is difficult, even under ideal circumstances . . .and of course, most wraiths have fetters to worry about. So, unless you deliberately want to include them in your campaign, you can safely ignore them.<br /> <br /> But basically, the Far Shores are an &quot;afterlife&quot; for the wraiths. Sort of counterparts of the Dark Kingdoms, each place in the Far Shore is themed after a different afterlife. No one remembers who built them (or if they're the &quot;real&quot; thing), but a wraith can visit Heaven, Valhalla, the Elysian Fields . . . or Hell, Tartereus, or worse. According to legend, the Far Shores exist to help wraiths pursue Transcendence, but few have been there and returned . . . and fewer still are willing to talk about what they saw there.<br /> <br /> Below the Sunless Sea, accessible only via the Veinous Stair, is the Labyrinth. No one knows what the Labyrinth is made of . . . and few want to. If a Dark Kingdom is made of memories that the living treasure, then the Labyrinth is crafted of things they desperately want to forget. Overflowing sewers, the gas chambers of death camps, the basement where a man did unspeakable things to children . . . The Labyrinth knows what scares you, and constantly shifts between horrors, with all the consistency of a nightmare.<br /> <br /> And at the bottom/heart of the Labyrinth are the Malfeans. Some wraiths whisper that the Neverborn are fallen gods, slain at the dawn of creation. Others speculate that they are the children of Oblivion itself. Either way, the Malfeans scheme endlessly to drag all Creation down, and into Oblivion . . . </span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","user":"One Vorlon","date":". 01-03-2015, 05:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T07:23:24-08:00","id":358299,"title":"","content":"Good expanation but Transcendence isn't the same as a Ascension. Ascension is an upward march towards... well god-like power and understanding of the universe. Transcendence is what happens once a wraith has completed the process of resolving their Passions, allowing them to move on as they were supposed to when they died.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T12:38:58-08:00","id":358521,"title":"","content":"Something I haven't seen mentioned in here yet as to why someone might want to play Wraith...<br /> <br /> It is the only game I have ever seen that could handle The Shoah.<br /> <br /> Charnal Houses of Europe: The Shoah is a book that could not have happened anywhere else. There are certainly game worlds that could make room for a holocaust, or even The Holocaust. There are also game rules systems that could model various parts of what happened during that time. However I do not think that there has ever been any other game world and system that could handle that particular topic with even a fraction of the dignity and respect that Wraith did, while still making it a playable game.<br /> <br /> There have been very, VERY few games that try to tackle topics this serious, and for good reason. And yet despite all of the possible pitfalls which are too numerous to even try to list here, Wraith as a game was (with the help of some amazing writers) able to actually deliver a setting smack in the middle of one of the most horrible tragedies that has ever befallen mankind.<br /> <br /> As a book Charnal Houses of Europe managed not only to avoid raising the ire of Holocaust survivors everywhere for making light of their suffering and loss, but actually was applauded its efforts for continuing to tell their story which must never be forgotten. A book that makes a game out of the death and suffering actually won awards from the community of people that it spoke about.<br /> <br /> Think about that for a moment. People who actually lived through the Holocaust are still alive, and by most accounts their experiences are sacrosanct. Yet this &quot;game&quot; managed to do what museums and documentaries had been struggling to do for years. It actually puts the players right up against the true horrors of World War II and makes them confront those events from the inside out. If the writers had slipped up by even the slightest margin they and Wraith: The Oblivion would both have been torn apart by the combined outrage of people spread across the entire world. Yet despite all of that it succeeded where no other game could have. No other game that I know of has the maturity to pull off something like that, or at least not half as well as Wraith did.<br /> <br /> Not every game of Wraith has to be that serious, and I have seen some very good but very lighthearted role play come out of Wraith. But it's capacity for handling those serious moments is unmatched by anything else out there.<br /> <br /> Wraith is a game about the deepest of wounds, yet it can heal.<br /> Wraith is a game about the most traumatic memories, yet it can turn us towards the deepest introspection.<br /> Wraith is a game about the darkest of nightmares, yet it can comfort the frightened.<br /> Wraith is a game about the most frightening terrors mankind can produce, yet it can calm the soul.<br /> <br /> I have watched people confront their own demons in a wraith game by putting aspects of themselves into their characters that they could not otherwise confront, and in most cases they did it without even realizing it until after the had already grappled with the parts of themselves that they were terrified to admit even existed.<br /> <br /> Games in general are a powerful tool for teaching, and healing, at least when everything lines up right with the game, the players, and the person running the game. But none of them are more powerful of a tool than Wraith.<br /> <br /> These are the stories we tell. They are our own stories even when we do not realize it when we start the game. Wraith is the game that lets us tell them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4663-themog","name":"themog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T16:14:35-08:00","id":358594,"title":"","content":"This post is taking the place of a massive screaming rant about how unhappy I am with Charnel Houses of Europe, as a Wraith fan and as someone who lost family to the Holocaust.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T00:23:40-08:00","id":358886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hawthorn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/330032-sell-me-on-wr20-wraith-the-oblivion?p=334346#post334346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So as another couple of questions that spring up from my casual reading of on-line sources about Wraith.<br /> <br /> 1) If you are a Wraith that finds itself in the Skinlands in an immaterial state, how easy is it for you to interact with the material world?<br /> <br /> To expand on this a bit, is it made easier by the intervention/involvement of other supernaturals? Can Mages with Spirit and Vampires with Necromancy maybe help you out? Just thinking of possible points of &quot;cross-over&quot; that might facilitate both PC and NPC actions and plots.</div> </div> </div> </div>Without using Arcanoi (the wraith equivalent of spheres or disciplines), a wraith can't do <i>anything</i> in the Skinlands (world of the living). They are ghosts, after all. All they can do is look. They can't hug their grieving wife, talk to their child, strangle their murderer . . . if they try, they just pass harmlessly through the living.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, a wraith can go almost anywhere. A wraith interested in the occult probably knows who <i>all</i> the other supernaturals are in the area, what they are, who they're scheming with . . . You can easily ward against wraiths, but in the modern day, almost no one does. So they've got an all-access pass to mage chantries, changeling freeholds, vampire safe houses . . .<br /> <br /> But <i>with </i>the right Arcanoi . . . ever see <i>Ghost</i>? <i>Poltergeist</i>? <i>Beetlejuice</i>? How about <i>Amityville Horror</i>? A wraith with the right skills can rip souls from bodies, hurl butcher knives across the room, even reanimate their old corpse and claw their way out of the grave<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4) So Wraiths from other Dark Kingdoms have different souls based on cultural beliefs basically?<br /> <br /> Does this also tie into the fact they get different ghostly powers? I noticed the 185K stretch goal talks about the Jade kingdom getting different powers. Are they alternate levels of the same powers or are they brand new power types? </div> </div> </div>Picking up on <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4449-eisenkreutzer\"><b><span style=\"color:#2379b5\">EisenKreutzer</span></b></a> explanation:<br /> <br /> A wraith is a wraith, regardless of where they're from. Terminology differs, and there might be some slight practical differences, but from a practical perspective, you can consider them the same.<br /> <br /> However, a wraith is also little more than a bundle of memories &amp; emotions, and their Arcanoi are an expression of that. So there are substantial differences between the Arcanoi practiced by the wraiths of the different Dark Kingdoms. There are some similarities due to circumstances - for example, travelling through the Tempest is a problem for any wraith regardless of origin.<br /> <br /> But other Arcanoi . . .well, each culture has its own ghost stories and traditions, stories about what ghosts are <i>supposed</i> to be able to do. And that's reflected in the Arcanoi the wraiths from each culture develop. Stygian wraiths group their arcanoi by general effect: unleashing their anger as telekinesis, or making the walls bleed, or hijacking a living person's body and going for a joyride . . .<br /> <br /> The wraiths of the Dark Kingdom of Jade (basically China), on the other hand, organize their Arcanoi by profession - the powers they'll need to pursue a particular career in the afterlife.<br /> <br /> Oh, and as for Dark Arcanoi? Those are the wraith equivalent of a kuei-jin's Demon Arts. Powers fueled by the Shadow (P'o, dark side of the soul), and not the healthiest skills to practice . . .<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><u>Edit</u></i>: I feel like I'm quoting someone. If so, sorry that I don't remember who to attribute it to","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-06T09:56:34-08:00","id":361407,"title":"","content":"By the way, if anyone's interested in the <b>Wraith</b> Kickstarter, it ends in just over an hour (2 pm EST). And, thanks to stretch goals, it now includes an anthology of seven short stories, a &quot;Handbook for the Recently Deceased&quot;, and the <b>Book of Oblivion</b>. $35 for the digital/ebook release, and $110 for the limited edition (leather bound) collector's release","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-06T11:00:59-08:00","id":361450,"title":"","content":"So I added my pledge to get the Physical Copy at $110 (actually $135 due to me living in the UK) in the last 5 minutes of this Kickstarter. Setting was just to interesting to pass up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}